---
title: Smoking and hip fracture in older adults, a cohort study
---

# Smoking and hip fracture in older adults, a cohort study

## Abstract

Smoking is associated with fracture. We followed 5,000 adults for ten years. Smokers had a higher rate of hip fracture (adjusted hazard ratio 1.4, 95% confidence interval 1.1 to 1.8).

## Introduction

Earlier studies [1] disagreed on the size of the association.

## Methods

Adults aged 60 years or older registered with a general practice were eligible and were recruited between March 2009 and March 2010; follow-up ran to 2019. The exposure was current smoking, the outcome was hip fracture, and confounders were age, sex, and body mass. Smoking was measured by questionnaire, and fractures came from hospital records. Recall bias was limited by using records. The study size of 5,000 gave 80% power to detect a hazard ratio of 1.3. Cox regression was adjusted for the confounders, and missing data were imputed. The protocol was approved by the University Ethics Committee.

```{note}
The analysis code is on request.
```

## Results

Of 6,200 eligible adults, 5,000 were included and 4,800 completed follow-up. Baseline characteristics are in Table 1. The adjusted hazard ratio for hip fracture was 1.4 (95% CI 1.1 to 1.8).

## Discussion

Smoking was associated with hip fracture. One limitation is residual confounding. The results apply to adults in primary care.

## Funding

Funded by a research council, which had no role in the study.

